import "core"
import obsidian("artifacts/catln-planner")
import obsidian("artifacts/catln-planner/Daily/2026-01-03.md")
import obsidian("artifacts/catln-planner/Plans.md")
import obsidian("artifacts/catln-planner/Daily/Ideas.md")
import obsidian("artifacts/catln-planner/Daily/2026-02-30.md")
import obsidian("artifacts/catln-planner/2026-01-06.md")


# Tests importing an Obsidian vault which runs the Day Planner community plugin beside the Tasks
  plugin. A checkbox of such a vault carries what the planner reads it as: the block of the day its
  line writes at its start, and the day it is planned for — the day of the daily note it is written
  in, or the day it schedules where it is written anywhere else.
  The vault asks every task of the Tasks plugin to carry #task, so the lines not carrying it are read
  as /Data/Note/Task/Simple and the ones carrying it as /Data/Note/Task/Obsidian, and the planner's
  reading is seen on both.
  The daily note writes a time in each form the plugin reads one, and in the forms it refuses; the
  three notes beside it are named for no day, for a day that does not exist, and for a day outside
  the daily notes folder, and so are planned for none.
  The notes are named as well as the vault, for the reason obsidian.ct names its notes: a vault does
  not import the notes it holds, so a golden test walking out from here would reach the vault and
  stop.
  What each line is read as is held by the goldens of the notes; the day and the span of it each
  block takes up are held by the calendar's golden.
#calendar
main{io -> IO} -> (){IO} =
  _<- exit(val= 0)
  ()
